{"id":"curly-girly","title":"Curly Girly","url":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Curly_Girly","rev":240093,"fetched":"2026-10-16","lead":[{"t":"p","c":[{"t":"b","c":["Curly Girly"]}," is an aesthetic marketed at young girls, heavily inspired by \"diva\" motifs, as well as 2000s celebrity and pop culture. The name is derived from the curled serif fonts and shapes used in this style, and is ornate, playful, and frilly. Curly Girly may also be referred to as Libby Lu Kid or Tween Diva."]}],"sections":[{"title":"History","id":"history","blocks":[{"t":"p","c":["The term was coined by Jack Grimes, and foundational research was conducted by Evan Collins, both of the ",{"t":"a","c":["Consumer Aesthetics Research Institute"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Consumer_Aesthetics_Research_Institute"},"."]},{"t":"p","c":["Curly Girly is primarily influenced by the ",{"t":"a","c":["Groovial"],"id":"groovival"}," aesthetic, mirroring its swirls, spirals, whirly patterns, and bold colors. Additional inspiration comes from the ",{"t":"a","c":["Whimsicraft"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Whimsicraft"}," aesthetic, which contributed to the 'down-to-earthiness' of Curly Girly."]},{"t":"p","c":["This aesthetic shares elements with many others that also originated in the 90s, including the broader design style ",{"t":"a","c":["Global Village Coffeehouse"],"id":"global-village-coffeehouse"},". Additionally, similar girly diva motifs are present in ",{"t":"a","c":["Shoe Diva"],"id":"shoe-diva"},", although more \"grown up\" in comparison to the child-oriented Curly Girly."]},{"t":"p","c":["In the years following Curly Girly's inception, new aesthetics that developed also share common elements with Curly Girly. These include the aspirational fame seen in ",{"t":"a","c":["McBling"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/McBling"}," and Parisian motifs seen in ",{"t":"a","c":["Parisian Girly."],"id":"parisian-girly"}," The ",{"t":"a","c":["Scene Tween"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Scene_Tween"}," aesthetic emerged as a counter-culture to the mainstream girly styles."]}]},{"title":"Visuals","id":"visuals","blocks":[{"t":"p","c":["The visual style of Curly Girly is characterized by flat, simple design and \"curly\" fonts and iconography, most notably the Curlz typeface created in 1995. Pinks, blues, and purples are often used colors, and hearts, stars, and flowers are common tertiary design elements. Young, trendy drawn characters are a hallmark of the style, especially in books and websites. Common themes include fashion, phones, and other traditionally \"girly\" activities."]},{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Fonts"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["Curlz MT"]},{"c":["Old Comedy"]},{"c":["Countryhouse"]},{"c":["Gigi"]},{"c":["Scriptease"]},{"c":["Dollie Script"]},{"c":["Absolute Blonde"]}]},{"t":"gallery","items":[{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/2/2c/Curly_Girly_Countryhouse.png/revision/latest?cb=20260214233338","w":160,"h":160},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/5/50/Curly_Girly_Old_Comedy.png/revision/latest?cb=20260214233243","w":160,"h":160},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/c/c8/Curly-Girly1.png/revision/latest?cb=20251112001444","w":160,"h":160}]}]},{"title":"Music","id":"music","blocks":[{"t":"p","c":["The music of the tween demographic between roughly 1996 and 2005 was primarily defined by the ",{"t":"b","c":["\"golden era\" of teen pop"]},", a commercially driven, highly polished genre that strategically targeted young audiences with catchy melodies, upbeat tempos, and relatable lyrics about young love and school life. The Curly Girly aesthetic was used in various marketing material and music videos for several artists throughout the late 90s and early 2000s."]},{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Artists"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["Britney Spears"]},{"c":["Belle Perez"]},{"c":["Christina Aguilera"]},{"c":["Lolly"]},{"c":["Jessica Simpson"]},{"c":["Mariah Carey"]},{"c":["Blaque"]},{"c":["702"]},{"c":["TLC"]},{"c":["3LW"]},{"c":["M2M"]},{"c":["B*Witched"]},{"c":["No Secrets"]},{"c":["Spice Girls"]},{"c":["Atomic Kitten"]},{"c":["Nobody's Angel"]},{"c":["Mandy Moore"]},{"c":["Hilary Duff"]}]},{"t":"gallery","items":[{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/9/9e/Curly_Girly_%2828%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251123182940","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Britney Spears- ...Baby One More Time"]}," (1999)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/5/5e/Curly_Girly_%2824%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251123122841","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Leslie Carter- Like Wow"]}," (2001)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/6/6f/Curly_Girly_Lolly.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260212034344","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Lolly- Viva la Radio"]}," (1999)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/f/f1/Curly_Girly_Mandy_Moore.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260209041107","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Mandy Moore"]}," (2000)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/0/0e/Curly_Girly_Annelise.webp/revision/latest?cb=20260215011547","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Annelise van der Pol- Over It"]}," (2004)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/1/19/Curly_Girly_Xtina.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260212033927","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Christina Aguilera"]}," (2000)"]}]}]},{"title":"Media","id":"media","blocks":[{"t":"p","c":["Movies and TV shows linked to the ",{"t":"b","c":["Curly Girly aesthetic"]}," are typically live-action teen or \"tween\" comedies and dramas from the mid-1990s and early 2000s that emphasize themes of self-discovery, high school social dynamics, fashion, and romance. They visually align with the era's vibrant \"",{"t":"a","c":["McBling"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/McBling"},"\" or \"Bold Pink\" aesthetics. The films and shows feature iconic Y2K fashion, including bold colors, pastels (especially pink), trendy accessories, and \"diva\" hairstyles, all of which are central to the Curly Girly aesthetic. Fashion often serves as a plot device for character transformation or expression. Similarly to musical artists, the Curly Girly aesthetic was used in marketing material as well as set design for movies and TV shows during this time."]},{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Movies"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["Clueless (1995)"]},{"c":["The Baby-Sitters Club (1995)"]},{"c":["Sabrina the Teenage Witch (1996)"]},{"c":["Romy and Michele's High School Reunion (1997)"]},{"c":["Mary Kate & Ashley films (1998-2004)"]},{"c":["Jawbreaker (1999)"]},{"c":["Zenon: Girl of the 21st Century (1999)"]},{"c":["Life Size (2000)"]},{"c":["Zenon: The Zequel (2001)"]},{"c":["Legally Blonde (2001)"]},{"c":["Josie and the Pussycats (2001)"]},{"c":["The Princess Diaries (2001)"]},{"c":["The Hot Chick (2002)"]},{"c":["Legally Blonde 2: Red, White, & Blonde (2003)"]},{"c":["The Cheetah Girls (2003)"]},{"c":["Pixel Perfect (2004)"]},{"c":["Stuck in the Suburbs (2004)"]},{"c":["Bratz animated films (2004-2013)"]},{"c":["The Cheetah Girls 2 (2006)"]},{"c":["Bratz: The Movie (2007)"]}]},{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["TV Shows"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["Sister, Sister (1994-1999)"]},{"c":["Clueless (1996-1999)"]},{"c":["Moesha (1996-2001)"]},{"c":["Pepper Ann (1997-2001)"]},{"c":["Sabrina the Teenage Witch (1996-2003)"]},{"c":["Two of a Kind (1998-1999)"]},{"c":["The Amanda Show (1999-2002)"]},{"c":["Sabrina: The Animated Series (1999-2000)"]},{"c":["Totally Spies! (2001-2008; 2013-2015; 2024-Present)"]},{"c":["Lizzie McGuire (2001-2004)"]},{"c":["So Little Time (2001-2002)"]},{"c":["That's So Raven (2003-2007)"]},{"c":["Winx Club (2004-2009; 2011-2019)"]},{"c":["Bratz: The Series (2005-2008)"]}]},{"t":"gallery","items":[{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/0/03/Curly_Girly_%2831%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251123191855","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Sabrina the Teenage Witch"]}," (1996-2003)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/1/17/Curly_Girly_Lizzie_Mcguire.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260209041145","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Lizzie McGuire"]}," (2001-2004)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/c/c3/Curly_Girly_%2820%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251123115120","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Sister, Sister"]}," (1994-1999)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/4/46/Curly_Girly_%2834%29.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260106195651","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Stuck in the Suburbs"]}," (2004)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/2/27/Curly_Girly_The_Hot_Chick.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260209044224","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["The Hot Chick"]}," (2002)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/2/2c/Curly_Girly_That%27s_So_Raven.webp/revision/latest?cb=20260216224622","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["That's So Raven"]}," (2003-2007)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/d/dd/Curly_Girly_The_Amanda_Show.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260216230247","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["The Amanda Show"]}," (1999-2002)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/8/8b/Curly_Girly_%2833%29.jpeg/revision/latest?cb=20260106195456","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Pixel Perfect"]}," (2004)"]}]},{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Literature"]},{"t":"p","c":["Many popular book series aimed at young adults and tweens in the 1990s and 2000s used cover art that heavily featured the \"Curly Girly\" or general Y2K aesthetic."]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["From the Files of Madison Finn"]}," series (2001-2006)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Lizzie McGuire"]}," series (2002-2005)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Lucky Stars"]}," (2001)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["American Girl"]}," magazines (1992-2019)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Mary Kate & Ashley: Two of a Kind Diaries"]}," series (1999-2005)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Amelia's Notebook"]}," series (1995-2015)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Amazing Days of Abby Hayes"]}," (2000)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Chicken Soup for the Girl's Soul"]}," (2005)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Alice"]}," series (1985-2013)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Barbie Reading Enrichment"]}," (2005)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["The English Roses"]}," series (2003-2008)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Precious Girls Club"]}," (2008-2010)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Candy Apple"]}," collection (1991-2011)"]}]},{"t":"gallery","items":[{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/9/9a/Curly_Girly_Barbie_Book.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260212030504","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Barbie Enrichment"]}," (2005)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/2/2d/Curly_Girly_The_English_Roses.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260212030825","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["The English Roses by Madonna"]}," (2003)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/a/aa/Curly_Girly_AG_Book.webp/revision/latest?cb=20260212031146","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["American Girl School Smarts"]}," (2000)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/e/e9/Curly_Girly_Chicken_Soup.webp/revision/latest?cb=20260216235547","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Chicken Soup for the Girl's Soul"]}," (2005)"]}]}]},{"title":"Merchandising","id":"merchandising","blocks":[{"t":"p","c":["Tween merchandising, makeup and tech design in the late 1990s and early 2000s focused heavily on ",{"t":"b","c":["shine, color, and a fun, playful aesthetic"]},", often featuring branded apparel and a range of scented beauty products, devices and accessories aimed at the preteen and teen demographics."]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["Dessert Treats by Jessica Simpson"]},{"c":["Juice Bar Body Spray"]},{"c":["Stuff by Hilary Duff"]},{"c":["Disney Princess TV (model DT-1350P)"]},{"c":["Bath and Body Works Art Stuff"]},{"c":["Naturistics cosmetics"]},{"c":["Groovy Chick apparel, accessories, & home decor"]},{"c":["Hotsie Totsie cosmetics"]},{"c":["Lip Smackers"]}]},{"t":"gallery","items":[{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/0/05/Curly_Girly_Naturistics.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260209035409","w":160,"h":229,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Naturistics ad featuring Alexis Blendel"]}," (2000)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/9/98/Curly_Girly_Hootsie_Tootise.webp/revision/latest?cb=20260209043220","w":160,"h":227,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Hotsie Totsie Cosmetics"]}," (2000)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/a/af/Curly_Girly_Dessert_Treats.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260209035823","w":160,"h":204,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Dessert Treats by Jessica Simpson"]}," (2004)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/f/f5/Curly_Girly_Lip_Smackers.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260212023253","w":160,"h":201,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Bonne Bell Lip Smackers"]}," (1997)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/1/17/Curly_Girly_Art_Stuff.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260212024031","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["'Art Stuff' line from Bath and Body Works"]}," (late 90s-early 2000s)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/2/21/Curly_Girly_%289%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251116170217","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Juice Bar Body Spray"]}," (late 90s-early 2000s)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/d/de/Curly_Girly_%2811%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251116175734","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Duff Stuff Hair Care"]}," (2004)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/a/a4/Curly_Girly_%2810%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251116174601","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Naturistics Sweet Lips Lip Gloss"]}," (Late 90s)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/3/30/Curly_Girly_%288%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251116165716","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Inflatable Furniture"]}," (late 90s-early 2000s)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/9/9d/Curly_Girly_%2814%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251116214435","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Hello Kitty mini tote bag"]}," (2003)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/a/a2/Curly_Girly_%2815%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251116215947","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Disney Princess TV"]}," (2004)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/1/15/Curly_Girly_%2822%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251123121153","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Groovy Chick"]}]}]},{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Toys"]},{"t":"p","c":["Toys with the \"Curly Girly\" aesthetic were popular in the late 1990s and mid-2000s. They featured ornate, playful designs, bright colors (pinks, blues, purples), the Curlz typeface, and motifs like butterflies and hearts."]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["Clikits"]},{"c":["Barbie"]},{"c":["Hit Clips"]},{"c":["My Scene"]},{"c":["Groovy Girls"]},{"c":["Shoezies"]},{"c":["Clueless Hands-Free Phone"]},{"c":["Betty Spaghetty"]},{"c":["Sky Dancers"]},{"c":["Bratz"]},{"c":["Polly Pocket"]},{"c":["Zodiac Girlz"]},{"c":["Tamagotchi"]},{"c":["Girl Tech"]},{"c":["Lil' Bratz"]},{"c":["'N Style Girls"]},{"c":["Flatsy Dolls"]},{"c":["Evergirl Dolls"]},{"c":["Pixel Chix"]}]},{"t":"gallery","items":[{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/7/73/Curly_Girly_Barbie_Clock.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260212032320","w":181,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Barbie Alarm Clock"]}," (2000)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/4/4c/Curly_Girly_%284%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251116160253","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Clueless Hands-Free Phone"]}," (1998)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/1/10/Curly_Girly_MyScene_Doll.webp/revision/latest?cb=20260212033147","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["My Scene Barbie"]}," (2002)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/2/2a/Curly_Girly_Clikits.webp/revision/latest?cb=20260215011343","w":161,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Clikits"]}," (2003-2006)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/4/41/Curly_Girly_%286%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251116162157","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Groovy Girls"]}," (1998-2019)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/b/bb/Curly_Girly_%285%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251116161552","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["'N Style Girls"]}," (early 2000s)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/e/ed/Curly_Girly_%2812%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251116212859","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Girl Tech Password Journal"]}," (1998-2010)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/0/07/Curly_Girly_%2813%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251116213331","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Tamagotchi"]}," (1996-2003)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/7/7f/Curly_Girly_Betty_Spaghetti.webp/revision/latest?cb=20260216213736","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Betty Spaghetti McDonald's Happy Meal"]}," toys (2003)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/9/9f/Curly_Girly_Groovy_Chick.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260216214440","w":160,"h":204,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Groovy Chick"]}," products in ",{"t":"i","c":["Argos"]}," catalogue (2005)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/9/92/556086360_821264280437854_1825670750192201544_n.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260216220147","w":160,"h":213,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Polly Pocket"]}," in ",{"t":"i","c":["Sears"]}," catalog (2005)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/b/bd/Curly_Girly_Color_Struck.webp/revision/latest?cb=20260216220813","w":160,"h":209,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Color Struck"]}," vanity from ",{"t":"i","c":["Hanover Accessories"]}," (1999)"]}]}]},{"title":"Retailers","id":"retailers","blocks":[{"t":"p","c":["Several retailers from 1996 to 2005 defined the Y2K aesthetic, catering to teens and young adults with playful, ornate, and feminine styles. Key stores included dELiA*s with its babydoll dresses and flare jeans, Claire's and their endless accessories options and Limited Too's clothes featuring glitter and bright colors. One of the most popular retailers associated with this aesthetic is Club Libby Lu, an experience based retailer marketed towards kids and tweens that provided makeovers, hosted birthday parties, and sold kid friendly beauty products. Girls could choose from a variety of packages for their makeover, complete with hair, makeup and nails. The store encapsulates much of what the Curly Girly aesthetic is all about, and Curly Girly has been previously been referred to as Libby Lu Kid by the ",{"t":"a","c":["Consumer Aesthetic Research Institute."],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Consumer_Aesthetics_Research_Institute"}," Although Club Libby Lu closed all stores in 2009, Sweet & Sassy continues to operate today with a similar retail concept."]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["Club Libby Lu"]},{"c":["Limited Too"]},{"c":["dELiA*s"]},{"c":["Claire's"]},{"c":["Lisa Frank Co."]},{"c":["Bath & Body Works"]},{"c":["Sweet & Sassy"]},{"c":["Velvet Pixies"]}]},{"t":"gallery","items":[{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/0/07/Curly_Girly_%2832%29.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20251123195116","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Club Libby Lu"]}," (2000-2008)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/2/29/Curly_Girly_Libby_Lu_3.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260216222140","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Club Libby Lu"]}," (2000-2008)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/f/f1/Curly_Girly_Libby_Lu.webp/revision/latest?cb=20260215032737","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Club Libby Lu Potion Station"]}," (2000-2008)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/3/37/Curly_Girly_Libby_Lu_2.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260215033052","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Club Libby Lu Makeover Brochure"]}," (2000-2008)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/8/86/Curly_Girly_%2819%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251123114408","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Sweet & Sassy"]}," (2004-present)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/9/97/Curly_Girly_%2817%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251116221027","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Velvet Pixies, a short-lived prototype store by Claire's, inc."]}," (1999-2000)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/2/2c/Curly_Girly_Velvet_Pixies_2.webp/revision/latest?cb=20260216223050","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Velvet Pixies"]}," (1999-2000)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/5/5e/Curly_Girly_Velvet_Pixies.webp/revision/latest?cb=20260215032842","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Velvet Pixies"]}," (1999-2000)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/c/c5/Curly_Girly_%2816%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251116220507","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Limited Too advertisement"]}," (1996-2009)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/7/70/Curly_Girly_Delias.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260212031506","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Delia's Catalog"]}," (1994)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/9/9b/Curly_Girly_Claire%27s.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260212031856","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Claire's advertisement"]}," (late 90s-early 2000s)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/8/88/Curly_Girly_Lisa_Frank.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260216223509","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Lisa Frank Clubhouse"]}," (late 90s)"]}]}]},{"title":"Gallery","id":"gallery","blocks":[{"t":"gallery","items":[{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/c/cb/Curly_Girly_%281%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251112002749","w":160,"h":160},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/e/e3/Curly_Girly_%2830%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251123190454","w":160,"h":160},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/7/77/Curluy_Girly_Britney.png/revision/latest?cb=20260212025346","w":260,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Britney Spears"]}," official website (1999)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/0/04/Curly_Girly_Polly_Pocket.webp/revision/latest?cb=20260212024848","w":205,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Polly Pocket"]}," Flash game (mid-late 2000s)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/d/d4/Curly_Girly_Icons.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260212025737","w":160,"h":160},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/b/bd/Curly_Girly_Barbie.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260212030745","w":160,"h":164,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Barbie"]}," (2005)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/1/16/Curly_Girly_%2829%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251123183921","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Barbie"]}," (late 90s-early 2000s)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/8/89/Curly_Girly_Barbie_Website.webp/revision/latest?cb=20260215013103","w":246,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Barbie.com"]}," (2005)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/6/6e/Curly_Girly_Barbie_Stickers.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260212025909","w":160,"h":258,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Barbie"]}," sticker sheet (2000)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/f/ff/Curly_Girly_%283%29.png/revision/latest?cb=20251116153859","w":160,"h":160,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Zodiac Girlz"]}," (2002-2009)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/4/46/Curly_Girly_Stickers.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260212034831","w":160,"h":211},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/1/11/